        def "F15_AllFourIndexingBlockersHaveCompilerAcceptedRewrites" (
            doc = """Every operator the accelerator refuses now has a rewrite the
compiler takes, and one tent kernel does all four.

MEASURED FIRST, WHICH CHANGED THE PLAN. Every blocker's index was traced to see whether it
folds:

    ScatterND        85   index DATA, updates DATA
    GridSample        8   grid  DATA
    GatherElements    2   index DATA
    TopK              1   K     CONSTANT

So the constant fold that cleared the `Tile` blocker does not apply to any of the first
three. An earlier reading of this file predicted ScatterND would be the constant
class-padding of the keypoint schema; it is not, and that prediction is withdrawn.

THE REWRITE. Bilinear interpolation is a separable tent vanishing beyond one pixel, so an
indexed read is a weighted sum over a FIXED range of integer positions:

    out[i] = sum_k data[k] * tent(idx - k)

At integer positions the tent IS a one-hot, so the two agree exactly rather than
approximately. The index moves out of the ADDRESS and into a MULTIPLIER; every address is
then fixed at compile time. The addresses were the objection and the arithmetic never was.

    rewrite            nodes   max|diff| vs reference   DFC on hailo10h
    DeformBounded        160   3.081e-15 (r=1)          OK
    ScatterOneHot        939   0.000e+00                OK
    GatherOneHot        1353   0.000e+00                OK
    TopKRank             915   0.000e+00 (N=64)         OK

Each ships a negative control that must fail: an offset outside the clamp, and a fractional
index, which are the quiet ways each rewrite would be wrong. The deformable one is proved
rather than sampled -- `weftspun/lean-deform-exact`, six theorems, zero admitted goals, each
depending on propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound alone.

RETRACTED WITHIN THE HOUR: TOPK IS REWRITTEN TOO, AND THE CUT IS NOT NEEDED. This entry
said TopK "should not be" rewritten -- that selection is inherently data-ordered, that
Hailo's own DETR cuts its graph at exactly that boundary with `parser.nodes` from Conv_195 to
Add_3451, and that 50 of the zoo's 127 configs specify start and end nodes. All of that is
true and none of it made the cut necessary.

Data-ORDERED is not data-ADDRESSED. Sorting needs to know which element is larger; it does
not need to compute an address from the data. Rank by counting separates the two:

    rank[i] = sum_j step(x[j] - x[i])     how many elements beat element i
    out[m]  = sum_i x[i] * tent(rank[i] - m)

Every index is a compile-time constant, `tent` is the same one-hot as the other three, and
`TopKRank` parses on hailo10h -- 915 nodes at N=64, exact against torch.topk.

WHICH REMOVES THE HOST HALF ENTIRELY. A cut is not free: it costs a USB 3.1 round trip per
frame and the existence of a host-side pipeline. With all four rewritten the graph does not
leave the part, so the partition this plan has assumed since the edge-NPU entry -- backbone
on device at 95% of wall, decoder on host at 4.7% -- is a choice rather than a constraint.

THE STEP IS WHERE EXACTNESS GOES, AND IT IS A PRECONDITION ON THE SCORES. A true Heaviside
is unavailable: `Greater` yields a bool the compiler must then carry, and `t/sqrt(t*t)` is
0/0 at a tie. So step is a clipped ramp `clip(t * 1e6, 0, 1)`, exact when scores are
separated by more than 1e-6 and blending two ranks below it. Both controls fire: a 1e-9 gap
disagrees by 0.999, and an exact tie collapses two one-hots onto one slot and leaves a rank
empty. TopK on ties is arbitrary anyway, but this is a different arbitrary and says so.

COST, MEASURED IN THE COMPILER'S OWN UNITS RATHER THAN IN ONNX NODES. Node count is the
convenient proxy: the compiler fuses and folds, and the edge-NPU entry already recorded 825
nodes becoming 336 layers. Translated to hailo10h:

    rewrite              onnx   layers   ratio
    DeformBounded         160       65    0.41
    TopKElimination       485      230    0.47
    ScatterOneHot         939      629    0.67
    GatherOneHot         1353      625    0.46

1,549 layers in total, against the 336 the backbone compiles to at 576. So the rewrites are
roughly 4.6x the backbone in LAYERS while the decoder they replace is 4.7% of wall -- and
those two numbers cannot be compared, which is the finding rather than a caveat. Layers are
not cycles: 207 normalization layers and 104 reduce_sum layers are the same count and not the
same work, which is why the histogram is recorded beside the total.

The layer mix says where the weight sits. ScatterOneHot and GatherOneHot are 207
normalization plus 104 each of feature_multiplier, activation and ew_mult -- the per-slot
blocks, and the reason both sit near 625. DeformBounded is 65 layers, small enough that its
8 instances are unlikely to matter. TopKElimination is 230 and there is one of it.

WHAT IS STILL NOT MEASURED, STATED PLAINLY. No schedule and no cycle count. `optimize()`
needs a calibration set and `compile()` needs both, and the profiler has never been run here
-- the same gap the edge-NPU entry admits about its own throughput figures, which divide by
40 TOPS at an assumed 30% utilisation. Whether eliminating the host cut is worth 1,549 layers
is therefore still unanswered, and answering it needs a calibration set rather than another
parse.

RETRACTED, AND IT WAS MINE: the claim that data-dependent addressing is architectural and
therefore cannot run on a dataflow part. The addressing observation was right and the
conclusion hung on it was not carried by any measurement. `KNOWN_BLOCKERS` says a GridSample
decomposition "produces GatherElements", which is true of the naive decomposition and false
of this one -- it produces no dynamic index at all."""
        )
        {
            custom uniform token subject = "T09_GgufAndHead"
            custom string date = "2026-08-23"
            custom string evidence = "gate_dfc_ops.py hailo10h: DeformBounded OK, ScatterOneHot OK, GatherOneHot OK; index-origin trace 85/8/2 DATA, TopK K CONSTANT"
            custom int blockersRewritten = 4
            custom int blockersRemaining = 0
            custom bool hostTransferRequired = 0
            custom bool costMeasuredInLayers = 1
            custom bool costMeasuredInCycles = 0
            custom int rewriteLayersTotal = 1549
            custom bool wasRetracted = 1
        }

        def "F14_TheKeypointModelClearsTheAcceleratorAtOneWindow" (
            doc = """The keypoint device half is compatible with the UGen300, and the
checkpoint's own default is the incompatible configuration.

Measured with `scripts/gate_onnx_device.py` at 576, antialias resize folded, against the
DEVICE_OPS allowlist:

    num_windows=2   868 nodes   23 operators   max|diff| 3.338e-06   outside: Tile x1
    num_windows=1   825 nodes   22 operators   max|diff| 3.934e-06   outside: none

Those node counts are the ones `logbook-edge-npu-and-the-anny-forward.md` already recorded
as REJECTED and PARSE OK respectively -- taken there on the plain backbone, and now taken on
the keypoint variant, which is the model that actually ships.

`RFDETRKeypointPreviewConfig.num_windows` defaults to 2. So the model as constructed exports
the graph DFC 5.3.0 refuses, and the single blocker is the Tile that replicates DINOv2's CLS
token once per attention window: "Unsupported concat over axis batch". At one window there
is no per-window replication, the Tile does not exist rather than being folded, and every
operator is inside the allowlist.

THAT IS A NARROWER RESULT THAN "IT DEPLOYS", AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS. What clears is the
DEVICE HALF -- backbone and projector, which the same logbook entry measures at 95.0% of
wall. The decoder does not and is not expected to: GridSample is deformable attention, which
Hailo state is unsupported with no plan to add it, and ScatterND is the keypoint-schema
scatter. Those sit in the remaining 4.7%, on the host. The chain partitions by kind rather
than by size, and this measurement confirms the device side of that partition for the
keypoint model specifically.

COST, FROM THE SAME ENTRY: losing windowing is 1.35x wall-clock, 238.4 ms against 175.9 ms
at 576. The numeric agreement here is 3.9e-06 against the port's own bound of 4.2e-3, so
dropping to one window is not a precision compromise.

TWO THINGS THIS RUN FIXED BEFORE IT COULD ANSWER ANYTHING. `rf-detr-cpp` had no pixi.toml,
no pyproject.toml and no requirements file, so neither gate could be run from a described
environment on any desk -- F07's shape in a second repository. And `gate_onnx_device.py`
defaulted its output to `/tmp/device_half.onnx`, which does not exist on Windows, so the
export reached the write and died with a FileNotFoundError that reads like a model problem
in a traceback. A go/no-go about hardware has to run on the desk the work happens on.

`num_windows` is now a gate argument rather than a default nobody reads, with
`device-gate-nw1` as its task."""
